---
/* About page, Alistair St Pierre + Local Service Rocket agency story.
   Sourced from Found by Friday brain dumps (journey + 2026.09.09 Q1-10 agency model). */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Picture from '../components/Picture.astro';
import { absoluteUrl, breadcrumbList, graph, webPageNode } from '../data/schema';

const title = 'Meet the Marketing Agency for Trade Shops | Local Service Rocket';
const description =
  'Meet Alistair St Pierre and Local Service Rocket: Google marketing for home service companies, fees from $500/mo that scale with you.';
const pageUrl = absoluteUrl('/about/');
const jsonLd = graph([
  webPageNode({ pageUrl, name: title, description }),
  {
    '@type': 'AboutPage',
    '@id': `${pageUrl}#about`,
    url: pageUrl,
    name: title,
    description,
    mainEntity: { '@id': 'https://localservicerocket.com/#business' },
  },
  breadcrumbList(pageUrl, [
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'About', path: '/about' },
  ]),
]);
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <main>
    <section class="hero container">
      <div class="crumbs">
        <a href="/">Home</a>
        <span>/</span>
        <span class="current">About</span>
      </div>
      <div class="eyebrow">ABOUT</div>
      <h1>Marketing for Owners Building Real Crews</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Local Service Rocket is run by Alistair St Pierre from Wellington, New Zealand. What we do is
        easy to say and hard to do well:
        <a href="/services/websites">websites</a>,
        <a href="/services/local-seo">Google rankings</a>, and
        <a href="/services/paid-ads">ads</a>
        that turn Google into booked jobs, and then grow with you once your company can handle more.
        Electricians are where we have the most proof. Other home service trades follow the same
        plan.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="container split">
      <div>
        <div class="eyebrow">WHO I AM</div>
        <h2>I still do the work myself.</h2>
        <p>
          I have always been the technical one. Building on computers since I was a kid, studying
          media design, teaching, then working in product and games. What I am actually good at is
          not marketing talk. It is looking at all the moving parts, your ads, your Google profile,
          your website, the way your phone gets answered, and working out which one is stopping you
          getting customers.
        </p>
        <p>
          I run this the way a good sparky runs a job. Contractors do the parts that can be handed
          off, like design and build work, off a clear brief. I set the standard and check the start
          and the end of anything that matters. Ranking on Google is a competition against the other
          shops in your town, not a checklist, so what goes out has to actually beat them.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="aside-card">
        <div class="aside-person">
          <Picture
            name="alistair-photo"
            alt="Alistair St Pierre, founder of Local Service Rocket"
            class="aside-photo"
            width={96}
            height={96}
          />
          <div>
            <div class="aside-name">Alistair St Pierre</div>
            <div class="aside-role">Founder · Wellington, NZ</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="aside-label">QUICK FACTS</div>
        <ul>
          <li>Based in Wellington, New Zealand</li>
          <li>Works with home service companies across the US, Australia and NZ</li>
          <li>Starts at about $500 a month</li>
          <li>Proves it works before asking you to spend big</li>
          <li>Google profile, ads, websites, and hiring</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="container story">
      <div class="measure">
        <div class="measure__spine" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        <div class="measure__copy">
          <div class="eyebrow">A NORMAL WEEK</div>
          <h2>Watch the numbers. Fix what slips.</h2>
          <p>
            Day to day I am watching one thing: is Google getting you more booked work. That comes in
            through your website, your Google profile, Local Services Ads and your ads, and it is
            never perfectly clean which one gets the credit. What matters is that the total is going
            up, and that somebody notices quickly when it is not.
          </p>
          <p>
            Most owners, or whoever is in their office, send a quick note on how many jobs got booked
            that day. I watch the trend. When the setup is solid and nothing weird is happening in
            your market, it runs quietly. When something slips, that is where my week goes. I am not
            going to fill your calendar with meetings, I am here for the result.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="measure__rail" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="container story">
      <div class="measure">
        <div class="measure__spine" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        <div class="measure__copy">
          <div class="eyebrow">HOW THE AGENCY STARTED</div>
          <h2>From Confused by Marketing to Running It for Trades</h2>
          <p>
            Before Local Service Rocket I was the tech and operations guy inside someone else’s growing
            business. No share of it, and no real marketing skill. Projects I had started myself before
            that had failed for the same reason. So I left, spent my income on learning ads properly,
            and found out how unforgiving it is. Get the targeting, the tracking, the offer, the way
            the phone is answered, the website, or the Google profile wrong, and nothing works.
          </p>
          <p>
            My first paying client was <strong>$500 USD a month</strong>. I did not build a sales team
            first. I got the work right, then took the next client. It is the same now: start small,
            prove it, grow with the owner.
          </p>
          <p>
            Since then that has meant taking owners from a few hundred thousand a year toward
            multi-million, and launching brands like <strong>Dry Duck</strong> from nothing to serious
            monthly revenue inside a few months, with the owner working just as hard as me. It only
            ever works as a partnership.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="measure__rail" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="dark-band">
      <div class="container dark-inner">
        <div class="eyebrow on-dark">HOW WE WORK DIFFERENTLY</div>
        <h2>Most agencies optimize for closing. We optimize for staying.</h2>
        <div class="points">
          <div class="point">
            <div class="num">01</div>
            <div>
              <div class="point-title">Prove it before you pay big</div>
              <p>
                You start at $500 a month so we have to show you something before the fee gets
                serious. Too many agencies burned owners chasing big setup fees. We earn the next
                dollar when you are ready to grow.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="point">
            <div class="num">02</div>
            <div>
              <div class="point-title">Straight talk, no hype</div>
              <p>
                Nobody can guarantee marketing results. What I can promise is a process that has
                worked before, honest timelines and costs, small budgets early on, and someone who
                will not stop until a problem is fixed.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="point">
            <div class="num">03</div>
            <div>
              <div class="point-title">I call the shots on Google</div>
              <p>
                You know your trade better than I ever will. Google is my trade. I stopped going along
                with owners’ hunches on it. If we cannot agree on the basics, we are not a fit, and
                saying so early beats a year of quiet failure.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="point">
            <div class="num">04</div>
            <div>
              <div class="point-title">Do the basics better than anyone</div>
              <p>
                Secret strategies are mostly noise. Almost nobody in your market has done the boring
                Google basics properly. Doing those extremely well is usually the whole game.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="container story">
      <div class="measure">
        <div class="measure__spine" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        <div class="measure__copy">
          <div class="eyebrow">WORKING TOGETHER</div>
          <h2>What onboarding actually looks like.</h2>
          <p>
            <strong>First call.</strong> I am listening for whether you are actually in. The simplest
            test: will you chase reviews and do the bits Google needs from your end? This only works
            both ways. I bring the leads and the systems, you sell the job, do it properly, and look
            like a real company online. On that call we go through your Google setup, find the biggest
            thing holding you back, and pick where to start. Newer companies usually start on one
            piece at around $500 a month. Bigger companies often hand over several at once, and we
            will talk honestly about why splitting SEO and ads between two agencies rarely works.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>First 7 days.</strong> The setup work gets booked in and most of it is done.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>First 30 days.</strong> We start seeing what that setup is doing. Sometimes things
            move within days. Sometimes it is closer to two months, especially for a newer company.
            That is the other reason the starting fee is low. I do not want you paying agency prices
            through the quiet part.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="measure__rail" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="container story">
      <div class="measure">
        <div class="measure__spine" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        <div class="measure__copy">
          <div class="eyebrow">PRICING PHILOSOPHY</div>
          <h2>Fees step up when your company steps up.</h2>
          <p>
            I would rather make more when you make more. The usual path: start with one piece, SEO or
            ads, at about <strong>$500 a month</strong>, get the free traffic working, then add ad
            management once it makes sense. When you are ready to spend real money on advertising, my
            fee goes up with it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Why? Because a shop doing $50k a month and a shop doing $200k a month with trucks, techs
            and payroll built around the phone ringing are two completely different jobs. Keeping
            Google performing when your payroll depends on it is where the pressure is, and that is
            what you are paying for. SEO still matters underneath all of it, but past a certain point
            your money does more competing in ads than sitting in a bigger SEO retainer.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What I will not sell you:</strong> a guarantee. I tried it once, with a big fee up
            front, and it made the relationship worse. A fee that grows only when you grow already
            keeps me honest.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="measure__rail" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="container split soft">
      <div>
        <div class="eyebrow">THE HARD CONVERSATIONS</div>
        <h2>Getting the calls is my job. Closing them is yours.</h2>
        <p>
          My side is a steady flow of good calls from Google. Your side is turning them into jobs:
          quoting, selling the right work, staffing, running the business. When the phone is ringing
          but the jobs are small or the quotes are not getting accepted, the problem is usually the
          sales process, not the leads. I will tell you that straight. The clients who stay with us
          for years are the ones who can hear it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="eyebrow">AFTER 12 MONTHS</div>
        <h2>How I hope you’d describe me.</h2>
        <p>
          Competent. Trustworthy. Fair. Easy to talk to. Fast. If that’s how it feels to work
          together, we’re doing it right.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="container story">
      <div class="eyebrow">NEW ZEALAND TIME ZONES</div>
      <h2>Oddly perfect for trade owners.</h2>
      <p>
        Depending on your coast I am about 3-6 hours out. Most owners want to talk marketing at the
        <em>end</em> of the day, which is morning or lunchtime here. So you can call after the last
        job, on the drive home, or from the couch, instead of fighting for a 9am slot with an agency
        that has already gone home.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="container story">
      <div class="eyebrow">THE SAME PLAN, YOUR MARKET</div>
      <h2>A proven build, adjusted to what you are up against.</h2>
      <p>
        Everything starts from the same plan: what belongs on the site, how the Google profile should
        be set up, how a new ad account gets built. What changes is your market. Who you are up
        against, how long they have been there, how big your average job is. If the obvious search
        term is locked up by a shop that has owned it for ten years, we win a related one first, build
        up strength there, and come back for it. Same plan, different conditions on site.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="container story">
      <div class="eyebrow">WHY AI SHOWS UP IN OUR WORK</div>
      <h2>Same standards, built much faster.</h2>
      <p>
        When AI tools got good enough I tested them properly by building <strong>Dry Duck</strong>:
        brand, website and Google setup from a very small brief. Same rules as always, just built far
        faster than the old WordPress-and-plugins way. It is not magic. It means we can make changes
        quickly without breaking the things that make the phone ring.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="container cta-block">
      <h2>If You Want Someone Who Actually Guards Your Leads</h2>
      <p>
        Watch how it works and what it costs, then book your onboarding call. The first call is about
        your business, not a slide deck.
      </p>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a href="/get-started" class="btn btn-primary btn-lg">Schedule onboarding</a>
        <a href="/case-studies" class="btn btn-secondary btn-lg">See client results</a>
      </div>
      <p class="fine">
        Or <a href="/contact">send us a question</a> · Wellington, NZ · Serving US home service
        companies
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .hero {
    padding-top: 64px;
  }

  .crumbs {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 10px;
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--muted-3);
    margin-bottom: 22px;
  }

  .crumbs a {
    color: var(--muted-3);
  }

  .current {
    color: var(--ink);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  h1 {
    margin-top: 14px;
    font-size: clamp(30px, 4.5vw, 46px);
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    line-height: 1.1;
    text-wrap: balance;
    max-width: 18ch;
  }

  .lede {
    margin-top: 16px;
    font-size: 17.5px;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--muted);
    max-width: 52ch;
  }

  .split {
    margin-top: 64px;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1.4fr 1fr;
    gap: 40px;
    align-items: start;
  }

  .split.soft {
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
  }

  .split h2,
  .story h2 {
    margin-top: 10px;
    font-size: clamp(24px, 3vw, 30px);
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    line-height: 1.2;
  }

  .split p,
  .story p {
    margin-top: 14px;
    font-size: 15.5px;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--body);
  }

  .aside-card {
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-panel);
    padding: 24px;
    background: var(--section-bg);
  }

  .aside-person {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 14px;
    padding-bottom: 18px;
    margin-bottom: 18px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline);
  }

  /* :global() because <Picture> renders the <img> from a child component, so it
     never carries this page's scope attribute. */
  .aside-person :global(.aside-photo) {
    width: 68px;
    height: 68px;
    flex: none;
    border-radius: 99px;
    object-fit: cover;
    display: block;
  }

  .aside-name {
    font-size: 16px;
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
  }

  .aside-role {
    margin-top: 2px;
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .aside-label {
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: var(--muted-3);
  }

  .aside-card ul {
    margin: 14px 0 0;
    padding-left: 18px;
  }

  .aside-card li {
    font-size: 14px;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--body);
  }

  .aside-card li + li {
    margin-top: 10px;
  }

  .story {
    margin-top: 64px;
  }

  .dark-band {
    margin-top: 72px;
    background: var(--dark);
    color: var(--dark-text);
    padding: 64px 0;
  }

  .eyebrow.on-dark {
    color: var(--orange-on-dark);
  }

  .dark-inner h2 {
    margin-top: 10px;
    font-size: clamp(24px, 3vw, 32px);
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    max-width: 640px;
  }

  .points {
    margin-top: 32px;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 18px;
  }

  .point {
    display: flex;
    gap: 14px;
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.06);
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
    border-radius: var(--radius-card);
    padding: 20px;
  }

  .num {
    font-weight: 800;
    color: var(--orange-on-dark);
    font-size: 14px;
    flex: none;
  }

  .point-title {
    font-weight: 800;
    font-size: 15.5px;
  }

  .point p {
    margin-top: 6px;
    font-size: 13.5px;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--dark-muted);
  }

  .cta-block {
    margin-top: 72px;
    margin-bottom: 72px;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .cta-block h2 {
    font-size: clamp(24px, 3vw, 30px);
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }

  .cta-block > p {
    margin-top: 12px;
    font-size: 15.5px;
    color: var(--muted);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .cta-row {
    display: flex;
    gap: 12px;
    justify-content: center;
    margin-top: 22px;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }

  .fine {
    margin-top: 18px !important;
    font-size: 13px !important;
    color: var(--muted-2) !important;
  }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .split,
    .split.soft,
    .points {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
